  "image_name": "11284d6b2a91905c307f245cc7d637a0.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how electronic energy levels split when spin–orbit coupling (SOC) and inversion-symmetry breaking (ISB) act in opposite orders. In both panels, a set of initially degenerate levels first separates into two branches, and then each branch splits again, but the labels show different physical interpretations depending on which effect is considered first.\n\nIn panel a, SOC first combines orbital and spin angular momentum into states labeled by total angular momentum, producing two manifolds, \\(J_1=l+s\\) and \\(J_2=|l-s|\\). ISB then lifts the remaining degeneracy within each manifold, creating \\(k\\) and \\(-k\\) dependent partner states with opposite signs. Small colored arrows beside the levels indicate the associated spin directions.\n\nIn panel b, the order is reversed: ISB first separates states according to orbital angular momentum \\(L(k)\\) and \\(-L(-k)\\), and SOC then splits each of those into spin-resolved pairs. The resulting labels explicitly track both orbital and spin character, with signs changing between \\(k\\) and \\(-k\\).\n\nOverall, the diagram emphasizes that SOC and ISB together generate momentum-dependent spin and orbital textures, but the conceptual level structure differs depending on whether one starts from \\(J\\)-states or from orbital-polarized states.",
